Sec. 20001. This section appropriates over $10 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, with funds remaining available until September 30, 2029, to improve military quality of life. Key allocations include $2.9 billion to supplement the basic allowance for housing, $2 billion for the Defense Health Program, and $1 billion for the sustainment and modernization of unaccompanied housing across the Army, Navy, Air Force, and Space Force. The section also provides $100 million each for tuition assistance and child care fee assistance, and $590 million to increase the Temporary Lodging Expense Allowance to 21 days. Additionally, it temporarily raises the percentage of value for authorized investment in privatized military housing projects from 33 1/3 percent to 60 percent and extends the authority for privatized unaccompanied housing until September 30, 2029.

Sec. 20002. This section appropriates approximately $25.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to enhance shipbuilding resources and industrial base capabilities. Major expenditures include $4.6 billion for a second Virginia-class submarine in fiscal year 2026 and $5.4 billion for two additional Guided Missile Destroyer (DDG) ships. The bill allocates $2.725 billion for the procurement of T-AO oilers, $1.8 billion for Landing Ship Medium procurement, and $1.534 billion for the expansion of small unmanned surface vessel production. It also funds $750 million for supplier development across the naval shipbuilding industrial base and $500 million each for additional dry-dock capability and the adoption of advanced manufacturing techniques.

Sec. 20003. This section appropriates over $25 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to advance integrated air and missile defense technologies. Significant allocations include $7.2 billion for the development, procurement, and integration of military space-based sensors and $5.6 billion for space-based and boost phase intercept capabilities. For layered homeland defense, the section provides $2.2 billion for the acceleration of hypersonic defense systems and $1.975 billion for improved ground-based missile defense radars. It also allocates $800 million for next-generation intercontinental ballistic missile defense systems and $2 billion for air moving target indicator military satellites.

Sec. 20004. This section appropriates over $15 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to bolster munitions production and defense supply chain resiliency. Key investments include $2 billion for improving the United States stockpile of critical minerals and $1 billion each for the expansion of the one-way attack unmanned aerial systems industrial base and the creation of next-generation automated munitions production factories. The section allocates $688 million for long-range multi-service cruise missiles and $630 million for long-range Navy air defense and anti-ship missiles. Additionally, it provides $3.3 billion for grants and purchase commitments through the Industrial Base Fund and $5 billion for investments in critical minerals supply chains.

Sec. 20005. This section appropriates over $13 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to scale low-cost weapons and innovative technologies into production. Major allocations include $2 billion for the expansion of Defense Innovation Unit scaling of commercial technology and $1.5 billion for the development, procurement, and integration of low-cost cruise missiles. The section funds $1.4 billion for the expansion of the small unmanned aerial system industrial base and $1 billion for programs to accelerate the procurement and fielding of innovative technologies. It also provides $1 billion to the Department of Defense Credit Program Account to subsidize loans and loan guarantees up to $100 billion for capital assistance.

Sec. 20006. This section appropriates $380 million to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to improve Department of Defense efficiency and cybersecurity. The funds are allocated for $200 million to deploy automation and artificial intelligence to accelerate financial statement audits and $150 million for business systems replacement to support these audits. Additionally, $20 million is provided for defense cybersecurity programs of the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, and $10 million is designated for improving the budgetary and programmatic infrastructure of the Office of the Secretary of Defense.

Sec. 20007. This section appropriates over $10 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to enhance air superiority capabilities. Significant expenditures include $3.15 billion to increase F-15EX aircraft production and $750 million to accelerate the FA/XX aircraft program. The section allocates $678 million to accelerate the Collaborative Combat Aircraft program and $600 million for the development, procurement, and integration of Air Force long-range strike aircraft. It also provides $500 million for Navy long-range strike aircraft and $474 million to increase EA-37B production.

Sec. 20008. This section appropriates over $15 billion to the Secretary of Defense and the Administrator of the National Nuclear Security Administration (NNSA) for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to enhance nuclear forces. For the Department of Defense, $4.5 billion is allocated specifically for the expansion of B-21 long-range bomber production capacity and the purchase of aircraft, while $2.5 billion is designated for risk reduction activities for the Sentinel intercontinental ballistic missile program. The NNSA receives $1 billion to accelerate facility construction and $750 million each for primary and secondary capability modernization. Additional funds include $2 billion to accelerate the nuclear-armed sea-launched cruise missile and $400 million for the warhead development of that missile.

Sec. 20009. This section appropriates over $12 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to improve the capabilities of United States Indo-Pacific Command. Major allocations include $3.65 billion for the development, procurement, and integration of United States military satellites and their protection, and $1 billion each for offensive cyber operations and the X-37B military spacecraft program. The section funds $1.1 billion for infrastructure development within the Indo-Pacific Command area of operations and $532.6 million for the Pacific Air Force biennial large-scale exercise. It also provides $500 million for the development, coordination, and deployment of economic competition effects within the Department of Defense.

Sec. 20010. This section appropriates over $18 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to improve the readiness of the Department of Defense. Key investments include $2.5 billion for Air Force facilities sustainment, restoration, and modernization, and $2 billion for Navy depot and shipyard modernization and capacity enhancement. The section allocates $2.118 billion for spares and repairs to keep Air Force aircraft mission capable and $1.64 billion for Special Operations Command equipment, readiness, and operations. Additionally, $1.5 billion is provided for Army depot modernization and $1.5 billion for increased depot and shipyard maintenance activities.

Sec. 20011. This section appropriates $1 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to support border operations and counter-drug missions. The funds are designated for the deployment of military personnel, operations and maintenance activities, and counter-narcotics and counter-transnational criminal organization mission support. It also covers the operation of national defense areas, construction in national defense areas, and the temporary detention of migrants on Department of Defense installations in accordance with chapter 15 of title 10, United States Code.

Sec. 20012. This section appropriates $10 million to the Inspector General of the Department of Defense for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029, to monitor Department of Defense activities funded in this title. The oversight specifically targets programs with mutual technological dependencies, programs with related data management and data ownership considerations, and programs particularly vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and long lead time components.

Sec. 20013. This section authorizes appropriations for military construction, land acquisition, and military family housing functions of each military department as specified in the title. It requires the Secretary of each military department to submit a detailed spending plan by project to the Senate and House Committees on Armed Services within 30 days of enactment for all funds made available by this title for military construction projects. [end of text]


